{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DabBy28EYSFeu4kxMZ895H","type":"tool_result","content":"293-# `_e3_check_canonical_path_write` above matches WRITE shapes only. A canonical\n294-# path sitting in the SCRIPT position of an interpreter is not one of them, so\n295-#\n296:#     python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n297:#     ./.claude/scripts/night-mode.py on\n298-#\n299-# both returned ALLOW while three signed comments (check_canonical_edit.py's\n300:# `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` note, settings.json's `_night_mode_deny_surface_comment`,\n301-# and the round-3 sentinel) claimed the model rail could not invoke the toggle.\n302-# Listing the writer in `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` protects the FILE; this matcher\n303-# protects the ACTION.\n--\n321-#   • `_e4` (here)    → DIRECT argv forms: the path as the command word, or as\n322-#     the operand an interpreter/prefix runner would execute, plus the\n323-#     CREATION of an executable alias (`ln`, see `_E4_LINK_RUNNERS`).\n324:#   • `night-mode.py`'s own SELF-PATH GUARD → execution through an alias that\n325-#     already exists. A matcher sees spellings; an alias exists precisely to\n326-#     have a different one, so this layer is the boundary and the two above\n327-#     are the early signal. See the r2 note at the end of this comment.\n--\n347-# Both were found on the model's own draft, by the cross-vendor pair rail.\n348-#\n349-# CODEX S292 REVIEW ROUND 2 (P1 — executable ALIASES). The fixed matcher still\n350:# recognised only SPELLINGS, so `ln -s .claude/scripts/night-mode.py /tmp/nm &&\n351-# python3 /tmp/nm on` armed the posture: `ln` reads the toggle (not a write, so\n352-# `_e3` allows it), and at execution time nothing but `/tmp/nm` is on the\n353-# command line. Measured on this platform before fixing — the alias printed\n354-# `result=applied` and wrote `defaultMode: acceptEdits`. Re-probing widened the\n355-# class beyond the finding: a RENAMED COPY inside the repo\n356:# (`cp …/night-mode.py .claude/scripts/nm2.py && python3 .claude/scripts/nm2.py\n357-# on`) armed it too, and no `ln` rule would have caught that one.\n358-#\n359-# The fix is therefore split by what each layer can actually know:\n--\n361-#     Cheap, string-only, and the earliest signal. NOT sufficient: the alias\n362-#     can also arrive from a previous session, a `git checkout`, `os.symlink`,\n363-#     `tar -x`, or the operator's own hand.\n364:#   • IN THE SCRIPT — `night-mode.py` refuses to run unless the file it was\n365:#     invoked as IS the canonical `<repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`. That\n366-#     covers both members of the class, holds however the alias was made, and\n367-#     holds even if this hook never runs.\n368-# Doing the execution half here was considered and REJECTED: it would require\n--\n373-# The governance surface. One entry today; the frozenset is the seam for any\n374-# future posture writer (add the path here AND to `_CANONICAL_GUARDS` — the\n375-# coherence test in test_bash_posture_toggle_invocation.py fails otherwise).\n376:_E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS = frozenset({\".claude/scripts/night-mode.py\"})\n377-_E4_TOGGLE_BASENAMES = frozenset(\n378-    _e3_basename(_p) for _p in _E4_POSTURE_TOGGLE_SCRIPTS\n379-)\n--\n410-# (GNU bash 3.2.57, arm64-apple-darwin25), every one of these ran the toggle\n411-# while the matcher returned ALLOW:\n412-#\n413:#   python3 .claude/scripts/{night-mode,other}.py on   -> 2 words, argv[1] is the toggle\n414:#   python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode{,}.py on        -> 2 identical words\n415-#   python3 .claude/scripts/night{-,x}mode.py on       -> toggle + a decoy\n416:#   python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.p{y..y} on      -> ONE word: the canonical path\n417-#\n418-# The last one is the strongest and was NOT in the finding: a RANGE whose\n419-# endpoints are equal expands to a SINGLE word, so the command line bash\n420-# executes is byte-identical to the literal deny case — `argv` included\n421:# (`TOGGLE RAN