{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012dAT16wBNMYiYPVxrUtsJy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"re-pin-codex.py — generate the Codex CLI re-pin pack (ADR-182 §5).\n3\t\n4\tIt EMITS a pack; it never applies one.\n5\t\n6\tGiven a target ``@openai/codex`` version, the tool:\n7\t\n8\t1. resolves the main package and its platform artifact through ``npm view``\n9\t   against the public registry named explicitly (``--registry`` and\n10\t   ``--@openai:registry`` = ``https://registry.npmjs.org/``, whatever an\n11\t   ``.npmrc`` says; the sentinel records it and ``dist.tarball``). The platform\n12\t   artifact is the optional-dependency alias\n13\t   ``npm:@openai/codex@<ver>-<platform>`` (ADR-182 §5 step 2), and the alias\n14\t   must name exactly the requested version;\n15\t2. downloads the platform tarball with ``npm pack`` into a private temporary\n16\t   directory, or takes a local tarball with ``--offline-from`` (``npm view``\n17\t   still runs: the registry integrity is the thing the tarball is checked\n18\t   against). npm's own cache and logs point into that temporary directory,\n19\t   which is removed on return. The tarball is read ONCE, into memory, from one\n20\t   descriptor opened without following a symlink and checked to be a regular\n21\t   file; steps 3 and 4 both run on those bytes, so a file swapped or rewritten\n22\t   on disk afterwards cannot split what is checked from what is pinned;\n23\t3. checks the tarball's sha512 against the registry ``dist.integrity``. On a\n24\t   mismatch it stops before anything is written;\n25\t4. streams the single payload member at the manifest's recorded relative path\n26\t   out of the tarball (never written to disk) and sha256s it. Members are\n27\t   keyed where npm's extractor puts them (npm 11: pacote extracts with\n28\t   node-tar ``strip: 1``): the first raw path segment is dropped whatever it\n29\t   is (``package``, ``other``, or the ``.`` of ``./package/…``), then ``./``,\n30\t   repeated ``/`` and letter case are normalised. An absolute or ``..``\n31\t   member name anywhere, a second member that lands on the payload path, a\n32\t   payload that is not a regular file, and a link that lands on the payload\n33\t   path or one of its parent directories, or points at the payload path, are\n34\t   all refused. The two readers (Python's ``tarfile`` and npm's node-tar)\n35\t   must see the SAME member list, so the list is accepted only in the one\n36\t   header shape whose decoding the tool models for both: the tarball is\n37\t   decompressed once (bounded, single gzip member, nothing after the gzip\n38\t   stream); every 512-byte header is plain POSIX ustar (magic\n39\t   ``ustar\\\\0`` + ``00``), with a checksum both readers parse to the same\n40\t   value and that matches the unsigned sum, an octal size field, printable\n41\t   ASCII names (name, prefix, link name), and a type among regular file,\n42\t   directory, hard link and symlink (only a regular file has a size or a\n43\t   body; only a link has a link name; only a directory name ends in\n44\t   ``/``); the member list ends at the first all-zero block and every byte\n45\t   after it is zero; and Python's\n46\t   ``tarfile`` must list exactly the members that header walk lists. Any\n47\t   other header (an extension header of any kind, another type, another\n48\t   magic, a malformed field) is refused by name. The registry artifact\n49\t   (0.156.1, measured) uses regular files only;\n50\t5. writes a NEW pack directory (an existing path is refused) holding:\n51\t\n52\t   - ``codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new``: schema 1 with ``package_version``,\n53\t     the PLATFORM ``npm_integrity`` and the per-triple ``sha256``;\n54\t   - ``codex-cli-pin.txt.new``: the live pin's lines before the range,\n55\t     unchanged, plus a dated paragraph and the range. The range is widened\n56\t     upper-only, and only when the version\n57\t     falls outside it. The human rationale comes from ``--pin-note FILE``;\n58\t     without it the paragraph carries a ``TODO(owner)``;\n59\t   - ``<mold basename>``: the ceremony script. The mold's body is copied\n60\t     verbatim except the regions the generator owns (per grammar, below). A\n61\t     guard is added after the mold's EXIT trap that refuses a real run while\n62\t     a ``TODO(owner)`` remains in the sentinel or in the pin;\n63\t   - ``pin-<tag>-approved.md``: the sentinel draft. The measured digests, the\n64\t     new range and the sha256 of the live canonicals and of the two ``.new``\n65\t     files are filled in; the human sections are ``TODO(owner)``;\n66\t   - constants-block grammar only, when the mold's ``PACK_FILES`` lists them:\n67\t     ``rehearse-pin-<tag>.sh`` (the mold's rehearsal, body verbatim, header\n68\t     regenerated) and ``README.md`` (provenance, measured values, file list).\n69\t\n70\tIt never touches ``.claude/governance/``, never installs a CLI, never signs and\n71\tnever commits. Applying the pack is the Owner's ceremony (the emitted script).\n72\t\n73\tTwo mold grammars are modelled, chosen by shape and parsed fail-closed; any\n74\tother shape is refused BY NAME (that pack is then cloned by hand):\n75\t\n76\t- heredoc grammar (the ``PLAN-189/codex-pin-0155`` generation): five value\n77\t  lines ``D``/``SENT_LIVE``/``NEW_RANGE``/``NEW_VERSION``/``NEW_SHA``, each\n78\t  exactly once in the expected shape, and a ``git commit ... <<DELIM``\n79\t  message. The generator owns those lines, the commit heredoc and the header.\n80\t  The mold must run the ADR-182 §5 step-4 verification AFTER it applies the\n81\t  manifest and before it commits: ``check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin``\n82\t  against the installed binary, then a check for ``\"status\": \"verified\"``;\n83\t- constants-block grammar (modelled on the hand-built PLAN-193 pack,\n84\t  ``codex-pin-0156``, which this tree may not carry yet): ONE block from\n85\t  ``# --- constantes`` to ``# --- fim constantes`` of ``KEY=VALUE`` lines\n86\t  holding exactly the keys of ``_CONST_RENDERED`` (values the\n87\t  generator renders: plan, tag, versions, range bounds, publication date,\n88\t  triple, digests, ``BASE_*_SHA256`` = sha256 of the live canonicals,\n89\t  ``SRC_*_SHA256`` = sha256 of the rendered ``.new`` files, ``GA_TAG`` from\n90\t  ``--ga-tag``, ``D`` fixed to ``.claude/plans/$PLAN/codex-pin-$PACK_TAG``)\n91\t  and of ``_CONST_PASSTHROUGH`` (copied verbatim), none assigned again outside\n92\t  the block. ``PACK_FILES`` may list only files this tool emits. The body must\n93\t  check the frozen ``.new`` copies against ``SRC_PIN_SHA256`` and\n94\t  ``SRC_MAN_SHA256``, apply the manifest, run ``verify_pin`` (whose body calls\n95\t  ``--verify-codex-pin``), die unless it exits 0 and require ``verified`` with\n96\t  the pinned sha256, in that order, before the plumbing commit. The mold must\n97\t  sit at ``.claude/plans/<PLAN>/codex-pin-<PACK_TAG>/``. Its rehearsal must\n98\t  read the constants from its pack's ``OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` and name no npm\n99\t  platform other than the one of the mold's ``TRIPLE``. The generator owns\n100\t  the block's values, the header and the guard; the generated header carries\n101\t  no version, digest or tag literal, because the rehearsal refuses one\n102\t  outside the block.\n103\t\n104\tIn both grammars the files the mold reads (``SRC_PIN``/``SRC_MAN``) and writes\n105\t(``DST_PIN``/``DST_MAN``) must be the ones this tool emits and targets, and no\n106\tversion, digest or pack-path literal may survive outside the regions the\n107\tgenerator owns: a mold that carries version-specific text in its body is\n108\trefused rather than copied stale. ``DRY`` (the switch the guard reads) is set\n109\tby ONE ``DRY=...`` line at column 0, or by ONE ``case ... in`` / ``esac``\n110\tblock at column 0 with one ``<pattern>) DRY=0 ;;`` arm and one\n111\t``<pattern>) DRY=1 ;;`` arm whose pattern names ``--dry-run``; no other line\n112\tassigns it. A single-command step anchor (the manifest apply, the\n113\t``verify_pin`` run, the plumbing commit) may end in one\n114\t``|| die \"<message>\"`` whose message runs no command; ``|| true`` or any\n115\tother suffix is not the anchor.\n116\t\n117\tThe mold is never older than the newest pack. Packs are ranked by the\n118\t``package_version`` of the ``codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new`` beside each\n119\t``.claude/plans/PLAN-*/codex-pin*/OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` (grammar-independent).\n120\t``--mold`` must pin the newest version, and every other mold at that version\n121\tmust parse too; otherwise the run is refused and names the newest mold.\n122\tBuilding from an older mold would silently drop whatever the newer pack\n123\thardened. ``newest_mold()`` is the one predicate; ``check-substrate-drift.py``\n124\tcalls it for its recommendation.\n125\t\n126\tThe pack's plan is named, never inherited from the mold: ``--plan PLAN-NNN``\n127\t(or ``--pack-dir``) is required, and ``.claude/plans/PLAN-NNN-*.md`` must\n128\texist (an orphan ``PLAN-NNN/`` subdirectory fails the plan schema,\n129\t``PLAN-SCHEMA.md``); a missing ``PLAN-NNN/`` is created with the pack (and\n130\tremoved again if the write fails). A constants-block pack must go to\n131\t``.claude/plans/PLAN-NNN/codex-pin-<tag>`` (its script derives that dir). The\n132\ttag is THIS TOOL's rule, not a repo convention: ``X.Y.0`` -> ``XY``,\n133\t``X.Y.Z`` -> ``XY-Z`` (``0156``, ``0156-1``). A hand-built pack may differ:\n134\tthe PLAN-193 pack pins 0.156.1 under the tag ``0156``. A tag already\n135\tused under ``.claude/plans`` (a ``codex-pin-<tag>`` directory or a\n136\t``pin-<tag>-approved.md``) is refused, which also catches two versions that\n137\tshare a tag (``1.23.0`` and ``12.3.0``); so is an existing pack that already\n138\tpins the target version. A ``--dry-run`` writes nothing and skips these two\n139\tchecks, so it can re-measure an existing pack.\n140\t\n141\tMaintainer-only. ``scripts/install.sh`` copies every ``.claude/scripts/*.py``\n142\tinto an adopter repository, so this file reaches adopters; there it refuses\n143\t(exit 2) because neither ``install.sh`` nor ``upgrade.sh`` delivers\n144\t``.claude/governance/`` or the pack molds.\n145\t\n146\tDeclared residual: the payload path is copied from the pinned manifest, and\n147\tthe tool checks only that the new tarball holds one regular member there.\n148\tWhether the new version's launcher (``bin/codex.js`` of the main package)\n149\tstill runs that path is NOT checked; ``--verify-codex-pin`` hashes the same\n150\tmanifest path, so the blind spot is shared. The generated sentinel says so.\n151\t\n152\tUsage (``X.Y.Z`` = the target release; ``<newest mold>`` = the\n153\t``OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` of the newest pack, which ``check-substrate-drift.py``\n154\tnames)::\n155\t\n156\t    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <newest mold>\n157\t    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <newest mold> \\\\\n158\t        --ga-tag vA.B.C          # constants-block mold: the tag the re-pin follows\n159\t    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <...> --dry-run\n160\t    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <...> \\\\\n161\t        --offline-from ./openai-codex-X.Y.Z-<platform>.tgz --pin-note ./why.txt\n162\t\n163\tExit codes: 0 emitted (or dry-run computed); 2 input refused (version,\n164\tmanifest, pin, mold, ``--offline-from``, output path — including one that\n165\tcannot be written); 3 verification failed (registry or tarball data\n166\tinconsistent); 4 infrastructure (npm absent or failing, timeout, any other\n167\tfilesystem error). No other exit code is used: a filesystem error that no\n168\tstep names is reported as 4, never as a traceback.\n169\t\n170\tStdlib only, Python >= 3.9.\n171\t\"\"\"\n172\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n173\t\n174\timport argparse\n175\timport base64\n176\timport datetime\n177\timport errno\n178\timport hashlib\n179\timport io\n180\timport json\n181\timport os\n182\timport posixpath\n183\timport re\n184\timport shutil\n185\timport stat\n186\timport subprocess\n187\timport sys\n188\timport tarfile\n189\timport tempfile\n190\timport textwrap\n191\timport zlib\n192\tfrom pathlib import Path\n193\tfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Set, Tuple\n194\t\n195\tfrom _repin_guard import GUARD as _GUARD, guard as _guard\n196\t\n197\tREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\n198\tPACKAGE = \"@openai/codex\"\n199\tGENERATOR = \".claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py\"\n200\tMANIFEST_REL = \".claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json\"\n201\tPIN_REL = \".claude/governance/codex-cli-pin.txt\"\n202\tMANIFEST_OUT = \"codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new\"\n203\tPIN_OUT = \"codex-cli-pin.txt.new\"\n204\tTODO_MARK = \"TODO(owner)\"\n205\t\n206\tEXIT_INPUT = 2\n207\tEXIT_VERIFY = 3\n208\tEXIT_INFRA = 4\n209\t\n210\t#: The registry every npm call names explicitly (never ~/.npmrc's choice):\n211\t#: the sentinel records it, so the signed reproduction runs against the\n212\t#: registry that was measured.\n213\tNPM_REGISTRY = \"https://registry.npmjs.org/\"\n214\t#: Both flags: a ``@openai:registry`` line in an .npmrc outranks ``registry``\n215\t#: for this scoped package, so the scope is pinned too.\n216\tNPM_REGISTRY_ARGS = (\"--registry=\" + NPM_REGISTRY, \"--@openai:registry=\" + NPM_REGISTRY)\n217\t#: Pack molds, relative to ``.claude/plans`` (the same glob the drift\n218\t#: detector used to walk).\n219\tMOLD_GLOB = \"PLAN-*/codex-pin*/OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh\"\n220\tNPM_VIEW_TIMEOUT = 120\n221\tNPM_PACK_TIMEOUT = 1800\n222\t# Cap on the tarball read into memory. The 0.156.0 darwin-arm64 platform\n223\t# tarball measured 131,567,699 bytes (2026-09-22).\n224\tTARBALL_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 30\n225\t# Decompressed-size cap (a gzip bomb stops here). Measured: the real 0.156.1\n226\t# darwin-arm64 platform tarball is 131,652,985 bytes gzipped and 326,415,360\n227\t# bytes of tar (2026-09-23).\n228\tUNPACKED_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 30\n229\t\n230\t# npm platform suffix -> launcher targetTriple. Mirror of\n231\t# check_pair_rail._codex_target_triple (the host -> triple map).\n232\tPLATFORM_TRIPLE: Dict[str, str] = {\n233\t    \"darwin-arm64\": \"aarch64-apple-darwin\",\n234\t    \"darwin-x64\": \"x86_64-apple-darwin\",\n235\t    \"linux-x64\": \"x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\",\n236\t    \"linux-arm64\": \"aarch64-unknown-linux-musl\",\n237\t    \"win32-x64\": \"x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\",\n238\t    \"win32-arm64\": \"aarch64-pc-windows-msvc\",\n239\t}\n240\t\n241\t_MANIFEST_KEYS = frozenset({\"schema\", \"package_version\", \"npm_integrity\", \"payloads\"})\n242\t_SEMVER_RE = re.compile(r\"(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\")\n243\t_RANGE_RE = re.compile(r\">=([0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)(?:,<([0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+))?\")\n244\t_SHA256_RE = re.compile(r\"[0-9a-f]{64}\")\n245\t_SRI512_RE = re.compile(r\"sha512-[A-Za-z0-9+/]{86}==\")\n246\t_PACK_DIR_RE = re.compile(r\"\\.claude/plans/(PLAN-[0-9]{3})/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*\")\n247\t_PLAN_RE = re.compile(r\"PLAN-[0-9]{3}\")\n248\t_DATE_RE = re.compile(r\"[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}\")\n249\t# Registry data is untrusted: only a plain https tarball URL reaches the sentinel.\n250\t_TARBALL_URL_RE = re.compile(r\"https://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._~@%+/-]+\\.tgz\")\n251\t\n252\t# Mold grammar (the value lines the generator owns, each exactly once).\n253\t_OWNED_SHAPES: Dict[str, \"re.Pattern[str]\"] = {\n254\t    \"D\": re.compile(r\"\\.claude/plans/[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+\"),\n255\t    \"SENT_LIVE\": re.compile(r'\"\\$D/pin-[a-z0-9-]+-approved\\.md\"'),\n256\t    \"NEW_RANGE\": re.compile(r\"'>=[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+,<[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+'\"),\n257\t    \"NEW_VERSION\": re.compile(r\"\"\"'\"package_version\": \"[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\"'\"\"\"),\n258\t    \"NEW_SHA\": re.compile(r\"'[0-9a-f]{64}'\"),\n259\t}\n260\t# Lines the mold must carry verbatim: what it reads is what this tool emits,\n261\t# what it writes is what this tool targets.\n262\t_REQUIRED_LINES = (\n263\t    'SRC_PIN=\"$D/' + PIN_OUT + '\"',\n264\t    'SRC_MAN=\"$D/' + MANIFEST_OUT + '\"',\n265\t    \"DST_PIN=\" + PIN_REL,\n266\t    \"DST_MAN=\" + MANIFEST_REL,\n267\t)\n268\t# Names the injected guard and the generated commit message rely on (``DRY``\n269\t# is checked by ``_dry_definition``: it has two accepted shapes).\n270\t_REQUIRED_DEFS = (\n271\t    re.compile(r\"^SENT=\"), re.compile(r\"^die\\(\\) *\\{\"), re.compile(r\"^HEAD_SHA=\"),\n272\t)\n273\t# ``DRY``, the switch the injected guard reads, in one of two shapes: ONE\n274\t# ``DRY=...`` line at column 0, or ONE ``case ... in`` / ``esac`` block at\n275\t# column 0 (no other column-0 code line inside it) whose arms\n276\t# ``<pattern>) DRY=0 ;;`` and ``<pattern>) DRY=1 ;;`` each appear once, the\n277\t# ``DRY=1`` arm's pattern naming ``--dry-run`` and the ``DRY=0`` arm's not.\n278\t# No other code line may assign ``DRY``. The shape is textual: what the\n279\t# other arms do (the PLAN-193 mold's ``*)`` arm exits) is not checked; an\n280\t# unset ``DRY`` makes the guard's ``\"$DRY\"`` abort under ``set -u``.\n281\t_DRY_LINE_RE = re.compile(r\"DRY=.*\")\n282\t_CASE_OPEN_RE = re.compile(r\"case .+ in\")\n283\t_CASE_CLOSE_RE = re.compile(r\"esac\")\n284\t_DRY_ARM_RE = re.compile(r\" +([^ ()][^()]*)\\) DRY=([01]) ;;\")\n285\t# A single-command step anchor may carry ONE trailing failure handler that\n286\t# dies: ``<command> || die \"<message>\"``. The message may expand ``$NAME`` or\n287\t# ``${NAME}`` but runs no command (no backquote, no ``$(``). Any other suffix\n288\t# (``|| true``, a second command) is not the anchor and is refused.\n289\t_OR_DIE = r'(?: \\|\\| die \"(?:[^\"`$\\\\]|\\$[A-Za-z_{])*\")?'\n290\t# ADR-182 §5 step 4, in this order (code lines, stripped): apply the new\n291\t# manifest, verify the INSTALLED binary against it, require \"verified\".\n292\t_APPLY_MAN_RE = re.compile(r'cp \"\\$SRC_MAN\" \"\\$DST_MAN\"' + _OR_DIE)\n293\t_VERIFY_PIN_RE = re.compile(\n294\t    r'python3 \\.claude/hooks/check_pair_rail\\.py --verify-codex-pin '\n295\t    r'\"\\$\\(command -v codex\\)\"(?: .*)?')\n296\t_VERIFIED_RE = re.compile(r\"\"\"grep -q '\"status\": \"verified\"' .+\\|\\|.*\\bdie\\b.*\"\"\")\n297\tGRAMMAR_HEREDOC = \"heredoc grammar\"\n298\tGRAMMAR_CONSTANTS = \"constants-block grammar\"\n299\t_SET_E_RE = re.compile(r\"set -euo pipefail\\s*\")\n300\t_HEREDOC_RE = re.compile(r\"git commit\\b.*<<\\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\\s*\")"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e0cc364-80e9-432f-8fee-77cee84da142","uuid":"35665c28-25cb-49db-a2dc-d88e5298f3a5","timestamp":"2026-11-13T18:45:34.133Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"re-pin-codex.py — generate the Codex CLI re-pin pack (ADR-182 §5).\n\nIt EMITS a pack; it never applies one.\n\nGiven a target ``@openai/codex`` version, the tool:\n\n1. resolves the main package and its platform artifact through ``npm view``\n   against the public registry named explicitly (``--registry`` and\n   ``--@openai:registry`` = ``https://registry.npmjs.org/``, whatever an\n   ``.npmrc`` says; the sentinel records it and ``dist.tarball``). The platform\n   artifact is the optional-dependency alias\n   ``npm:@openai/codex@<ver>-<platform>`` (ADR-182 §5 step 2), and the alias\n   must name exactly the requested version;\n2. downloads the platform tarball with ``npm pack`` into a private temporary\n   directory, or takes a local tarball with ``--offline-from`` (``npm view``\n   still runs: the registry integrity is the thing the tarball is checked\n   against). npm's own cache and logs point into that temporary directory,\n   which is removed on return. The tarball is read ONCE, into memory, from one\n   descriptor opened without following a symlink and checked to be a regular\n   file; steps 3 and 4 both run on those bytes, so a file swapped or rewritten\n   on disk afterwards cannot split what is checked from what is pinned;\n3. checks the tarball's sha512 against the registry ``dist.integrity``. On a\n   mismatch it stops before anything is written;\n4. streams the single payload member at the manifest's recorded relative path\n   out of the tarball (never written to disk) and sha256s it. Members are\n   keyed where npm's extractor puts them (npm 11: pacote extracts with\n   node-tar ``strip: 1``): the first raw path segment is dropped whatever it\n   is (``package``, ``other``, or the ``.`` of ``./package/…``), then ``./``,\n   repeated ``/`` and letter case are normalised. An absolute or ``..``\n   member name anywhere, a second member that lands on the payload path, a\n   payload that is not a regular file, and a link that lands on the payload\n   path or one of its parent directories, or points at the payload path, are\n   all refused. The two readers (Python's ``tarfile`` and npm's node-tar)\n   must see the SAME member list, so the list is accepted only in the one\n   header shape whose decoding the tool models for both: the tarball is\n   decompressed once (bounded, single gzip member, nothing after the gzip\n   stream); every 512-byte header is plain POSIX ustar (magic\n   ``ustar\\\\0`` + ``00``), with a checksum both readers parse to the same\n   value and that matches the unsigned sum, an octal size field, printable\n   ASCII names (name, prefix, link name), and a type among regular file,\n   directory, hard link and symlink (only a regular file has a size or a\n   body; only a link has a link name; only a directory name ends in\n   ``/``); the member list ends at the first all-zero block and every byte\n   after it is zero; and Python's\n   ``tarfile`` must list exactly the members that header walk lists. Any\n   other header (an extension header of any kind, another type, another\n   magic, a malformed field) is refused by name. The registry artifact\n   (0.156.1, measured) uses regular files only;\n5. writes a NEW pack directory (an existing path is refused) holding:\n\n   - ``codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new``: schema 1 with ``package_version``,\n     the PLATFORM ``npm_integrity`` and the per-triple ``sha256``;\n   - ``codex-cli-pin.txt.new``: the live pin's lines before the range,\n     unchanged, plus a dated paragraph and the range. The range is widened\n     upper-only, and only when the version\n     falls outside it. The human rationale comes from ``--pin-note FILE``;\n     without it the paragraph carries a ``TODO(owner)``;\n   - ``<mold basename>``: the ceremony script. The mold's body is copied\n     verbatim except the regions the generator owns (per grammar, below). A\n     guard is added after the mold's EXIT trap that refuses a real run while\n     a ``TODO(owner)`` remains in the sentinel or in the pin;\n   - ``pin-<tag>-approved.md``: the sentinel draft. The measured digests, the\n     new range and the sha256 of the live canonicals and of the two ``.new``\n     files are filled in; the human sections are ``TODO(owner)``;\n   - constants-block grammar only, when the mold's ``PACK_FILES`` lists them:\n     ``rehearse-pin-<tag>.sh`` (the mold's rehearsal, body verbatim, header\n     regenerated) and ``README.md`` (provenance, measured values, file list).\n\nIt never touches ``.claude/governance/``, never installs a CLI, never signs and\nnever commits. Applying the pack is the Owner's ceremony (the emitted script).\n\nTwo mold grammars are modelled, chosen by shape and parsed fail-closed; any\nother shape is refused BY NAME (that pack is then cloned by hand):\n\n- heredoc grammar (the ``PLAN-189/codex-pin-0155`` generation): five value\n  lines ``D``/``SENT_LIVE``/``NEW_RANGE``/``NEW_VERSION``/``NEW_SHA``, each\n  exactly once in the expected shape, and a ``git commit ... <<DELIM``\n  message. The generator owns those lines, the commit heredoc and the header.\n  The mold must run the ADR-182 §5 step-4 verification AFTER it applies the\n  manifest and before it commits: ``check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin``\n  against the installed binary, then a check for ``\"status\": \"verified\"``;\n- constants-block grammar (modelled on the hand-built PLAN-193 pack,\n  ``codex-pin-0156``, which this tree may not carry yet): ONE block from\n  ``# --- constantes`` to ``# --- fim constantes`` of ``KEY=VALUE`` lines\n  holding exactly the keys of ``_CONST_RENDERED`` (values the\n  generator renders: plan, tag, versions, range bounds, publication date,\n  triple, digests, ``BASE_*_SHA256`` = sha256 of the live canonicals,\n  ``SRC_*_SHA256`` = sha256 of the rendered ``.new`` files, ``GA_TAG`` from\n  ``--ga-tag``, ``D`` fixed to ``.claude/plans/$PLAN/codex-pin-$PACK_TAG``)\n  and of ``_CONST_PASSTHROUGH`` (copied verbatim), none assigned again outside\n  the block. ``PACK_FILES`` may list only files this tool emits. The body must\n  check the frozen ``.new`` copies against ``SRC_PIN_SHA256`` and\n  ``SRC_MAN_SHA256``, apply the manifest, run ``verify_pin`` (whose body calls\n  ``--verify-codex-pin``), die unless it exits 0 and require ``verified`` with\n  the pinned sha256, in that order, before the plumbing commit. The mold must\n  sit at ``.claude/plans/<PLAN>/codex-pin-<PACK_TAG>/``. Its rehearsal must\n  read the constants from its pack's ``OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` and name no npm\n  platform other than the one of the mold's ``TRIPLE``. The generator owns\n  the block's values, the header and the guard; the generated header carries\n  no version, digest or tag literal, because the rehearsal refuses one\n  outside the block.\n\nIn both grammars the files the mold reads (``SRC_PIN``/``SRC_MAN``) and writes\n(``DST_PIN``/``DST_MAN``) must be the ones this tool emits and targets, and no\nversion, digest or pack-path literal may survive outside the regions the\ngenerator owns: a mold that carries version-specific text in its body is\nrefused rather than copied stale. ``DRY`` (the switch the guard reads) is set\nby ONE ``DRY=...`` line at column 0, or by ONE ``case ... in`` / ``esac``\nblock at column 0 with one ``<pattern>) DRY=0 ;;`` arm and one\n``<pattern>) DRY=1 ;;`` arm whose pattern names ``--dry-run``; no other line\nassigns it. A single-command step anchor (the manifest apply, the\n``verify_pin`` run, the plumbing commit) may end in one\n``|| die \"<message>\"`` whose message runs no command; ``|| true`` or any\nother suffix is not the anchor.\n\nThe mold is never older than the newest pack. Packs are ranked by the\n``package_version`` of the ``codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new`` beside each\n``.claude/plans/PLAN-*/codex-pin*/OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` (grammar-independent).\n``--mold`` must pin the newest version, and every other mold at that version\nmust parse too; otherwise the run is refused and names the newest mold.\nBuilding from an older mold would silently drop whatever the newer pack\nhardened. ``newest_mold()`` is the one predicate; ``check-substrate-drift.py``\ncalls it for its recommendation.\n\nThe pack's plan is named, never inherited from the mold: ``--plan PLAN-NNN``\n(or ``--pack-dir``) is required, and ``.claude/plans/PLAN-NNN-*.md`` must\nexist (an orphan ``PLAN-NNN/`` subdirectory fails the plan schema,\n``PLAN-SCHEMA.md``); a missing ``PLAN-NNN/`` is created with the pack (and\nremoved again if the write fails). A constants-block pack must go to\n``.claude/plans/PLAN-NNN/codex-pin-<tag>`` (its script derives that dir). The\ntag is THIS TOOL's rule, not a repo convention: ``X.Y.0`` -> ``XY``,\n``X.Y.Z`` -> ``XY-Z`` (``0156``, ``0156-1``). A hand-built pack may differ:\nthe PLAN-193 pack pins 0.156.1 under the tag ``0156``. A tag already\nused under ``.claude/plans`` (a ``codex-pin-<tag>`` directory or a\n``pin-<tag>-approved.md``) is refused, which also catches two versions that\nshare a tag (``1.23.0`` and ``12.3.0``); so is an existing pack that already\npins the target version. A ``--dry-run`` writes nothing and skips these two\nchecks, so it can re-measure an existing pack.\n\nMaintainer-only. ``scripts/install.sh`` copies every ``.claude/scripts/*.py``\ninto an adopter repository, so this file reaches adopters; there it refuses\n(exit 2) because neither ``install.sh`` nor ``upgrade.sh`` delivers\n``.claude/governance/`` or the pack molds.\n\nDeclared residual: the payload path is copied from the pinned manifest, and\nthe tool checks only that the new tarball holds one regular member there.\nWhether the new version's launcher (``bin/codex.js`` of the main package)\nstill runs that path is NOT checked; ``--verify-codex-pin`` hashes the same\nmanifest path, so the blind spot is shared. The generated sentinel says so.\n\nUsage (``X.Y.Z`` = the target release; ``<newest mold>`` = the\n``OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh`` of the newest pack, which ``check-substrate-drift.py``\nnames)::\n\n    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <newest mold>\n    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <newest mold> \\\\\n        --ga-tag vA.B.C          # constants-block mold: the tag the re-pin follows\n    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <...> --dry-run\n    python3 .claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py X.Y.Z --plan PLAN-NNN --mold <...> \\\\\n        --offline-from ./openai-codex-X.Y.Z-<platform>.tgz --pin-note ./why.txt\n\nExit codes: 0 emitted (or dry-run computed); 2 input refused (version,\nmanifest, pin, mold, ``--offline-from``, output path — including one that\ncannot be written); 3 verification failed (registry or tarball data\ninconsistent); 4 infrastructure (npm absent or failing, timeout, any other\nfilesystem error). No other exit code is used: a filesystem error that no\nstep names is reported as 4, never as a traceback.\n\nStdlib only, Python >= 3.9.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport base64\nimport datetime\nimport errno\nimport hashlib\nimport io\nimport json\nimport os\nimport posixpath\nimport re\nimport shutil\nimport stat\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport tarfile\nimport tempfile\nimport textwrap\nimport zlib\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Set, Tuple\n\nfrom _repin_guard import GUARD as _GUARD, guard as _guard\n\nREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]\nPACKAGE = \"@openai/codex\"\nGENERATOR = \".claude/scripts/re-pin-codex.py\"\nMANIFEST_REL = \".claude/governance/codex-cli-pin-manifest.json\"\nPIN_REL = \".claude/governance/codex-cli-pin.txt\"\nMANIFEST_OUT = \"codex-cli-pin-manifest.json.new\"\nPIN_OUT = \"codex-cli-pin.txt.new\"\nTODO_MARK = \"TODO(owner)\"\n\nEXIT_INPUT = 2\nEXIT_VERIFY = 3\nEXIT_INFRA = 4\n\n#: The registry every npm call names explicitly (never ~/.npmrc's choice):\n#: the sentinel records it, so the signed reproduction runs against the\n#: registry that was measured.\nNPM_REGISTRY = \"https://registry.npmjs.org/\"\n#: Both flags: a ``@openai:registry`` line in an .npmrc outranks ``registry``\n#: for this scoped package, so the scope is pinned too.\nNPM_REGISTRY_ARGS = (\"--registry=\" + NPM_REGISTRY, \"--@openai:registry=\" + NPM_REGISTRY)\n#: Pack molds, relative to ``.claude/plans`` (the same glob the drift\n#: detector used to walk).\nMOLD_GLOB = \"PLAN-*/codex-pin*/OWNER-PIN-SIGN.sh\"\nNPM_VIEW_TIMEOUT = 120\nNPM_PACK_TIMEOUT = 1800\n# Cap on the tarball read into memory. The 0.156.0 darwin-arm64 platform\n# tarball measured 131,567,699 bytes (2026-09-22).\nTARBALL_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 30\n# Decompressed-size cap (a gzip bomb stops here). Measured: the real 0.156.1\n# darwin-arm64 platform tarball is 131,652,985 bytes gzipped and 326,415,360\n# bytes of tar (2026-09-23).\nUNPACKED_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 30\n\n# npm platform suffix -> launcher targetTriple. Mirror of\n# check_pair_rail._codex_target_triple (the host -> triple map).\nPLATFORM_TRIPLE: Dict[str, str] = {\n    \"darwin-arm64\": \"aarch64-apple-darwin\",\n    \"darwin-x64\": \"x86_64-apple-darwin\",\n    \"linux-x64\": \"x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\",\n    \"linux-arm64\": \"aarch64-unknown-linux-musl\",\n    \"win32-x64\": \"x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\",\n    \"win32-arm64\": \"aarch64-pc-windows-msvc\",\n}\n\n_MANIFEST_KEYS = frozenset({\"schema\", \"package_version\", \"npm_integrity\", \"payloads\"})\n_SEMVER_RE = re.compile(r\"(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\")\n_RANGE_RE = re.compile(r\">=([0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+)(?:,<([0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+))?\")\n_SHA256_RE = re.compile(r\"[0-9a-f]{64}\")\n_SRI512_RE = re.compile(r\"sha512-[A-Za-z0-9+/]{86}==\")\n_PACK_DIR_RE = re.compile(r\"\\.claude/plans/(PLAN-[0-9]{3})/[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*\")\n_PLAN_RE = re.compile(r\"PLAN-[0-9]{3}\")\n_DATE_RE = re.compile(r\"[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}\")\n# Registry data is untrusted: only a plain https tarball URL reaches the sentinel.\n_TARBALL_URL_RE = re.compile(r\"https://[A-Za-z0-9.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._~@%+/-]+\\.tgz\")\n\n# Mold grammar (the value lines the generator owns, each exactly once).\n_OWNED_SHAPES: Dict[str, \"re.Pattern[str]\"] = {\n    \"D\": re.compile(r\"\\.claude/plans/[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+\"),\n    \"SENT_LIVE\": re.compile(r'\"\\$D/pin-[a-z0-9-]+-approved\\.md\"'),\n    \"NEW_RANGE\": re.compile(r\"'>=[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+,<[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+'\"),\n    \"NEW_VERSION\": re.compile(r\"\"\"'\"package_version\": \"[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+\"'\"\"\"),\n    \"NEW_SHA\": re.compile(r\"'[0-9a-f]{64}'\"),\n}\n# Lines the mold must carry verbatim: what it reads is what this tool emits,\n# what it writes is what this tool targets.\n_REQUIRED_LINES = (\n    'SRC_PIN=\"$D/' + PIN_OUT + '\"',\n    'SRC_MAN=\"$D/' + MANIFEST_OUT + '\"',\n    \"DST_PIN=\" + PIN_REL,\n    \"DST_MAN=\" + MANIFEST_REL,\n)\n# Names the injected guard and the generated commit message rely on (``DRY``\n# is checked by ``_dry_definition``: it has two accepted shapes).\n_REQUIRED_DEFS = (\n    re.compile(r\"^SENT=\"), re.compile(r\"^die\\(\\) *\\{\"), re.compile(r\"^HEAD_SHA=\"),\n)\n# ``DRY``, the switch the injected guard reads, in one of two shapes: ONE\n# ``DRY=...`` line at column 0, or ONE ``case ... in`` / ``esac`` block at\n# column 0 (no other column-0 code line inside it) whose arms\n# ``<pattern>) DRY=0 ;;`` and ``<pattern>) DRY=1 ;;`` each appear once, the\n# ``DRY=1`` arm's pattern naming ``--dry-run`` and the ``DRY=0`` arm's not.\n# No other code line may assign ``DRY``. The shape is textual: what the\n# other arms do (the PLAN-193 mold's ``*)`` arm exits) is not checked; an\n# unset ``DRY`` makes the guard's ``\"$DRY\"`` abort under ``set -u``.\n_DRY_LINE_RE = re.compile(r\"DRY=.*\")\n_CASE_OPEN_RE = re.compile(r\"case .+ in\")\n_CASE_CLOSE_RE = re.compile(r\"esac\")\n_DRY_ARM_RE = re.compile(r\" +([^ ()][^()]*)\\) DRY=([01]) ;;\")\n# A single-command step anchor may carry ONE trailing failure handler that\n# dies: ``<command> || die \"<message>\"``. The message may expand ``$NAME`` or\n# ``${NAME}`` but runs no command (no backquote, no ``$(``). Any other suffix\n# (``|| true``, a second command) is not the anchor and is refused.\n_OR_DIE = r'(?: \\|\\| die \"(?:[^\"`$\\\\]|\\$[A-Za-z_{])*\")?'\n# ADR-182 §5 step 4, in this order (code lines, stripped): apply the new\n# manifest, verify the INSTALLED binary against it, require \"verified\".\n_APPLY_MAN_RE = re.compile(r'cp \"\\$SRC_MAN\" \"\\$DST_MAN\"' + _OR_DIE)\n_VERIFY_PIN_RE = re.compile(\n    r'python3 \\.claude/hooks/check_pair_rail\\.py --verify-codex-pin '\n    r'\"\\$\\(command -v codex\\)\"(?: .*)?')\n_VERIFIED_RE = re.compile(r\"\"\"grep -q '\"status\": \"verified\"' .+\\|\\|.*\\bdie\\b.*\"\"\")\nGRAMMAR_HEREDOC = \"heredoc grammar\"\nGRAMMAR_CONSTANTS = \"constants-block grammar\"\n_SET_E_RE = re.compile(r\"set -euo pipefail\\s*\")\n_HEREDOC_RE = re.compile(r\"git commit\\b.*<<\\s*([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\\s*\")","numLines":300,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2314}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012dAT16wBNMYiYPVxrUtsJy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
